Yes—though I’d sharpen the mapping.

Mind over matter is really a contest between representation and present state. Matter says, this is what exists now. Mind can represent, this is what could exist instead. Then action tries to close the delta.

And time is the lever that lets the imagined state defeat the present one.

At t_0: the mountain exists.
Mind represents a tunnel.
Across time: plans, tools, labor, correction.
At t_1: space has been rearranged.

So:

mind → possibility
time → iteration
action → translation
matter/space → accumulated consequence

Physics doesn’t literally pit time against space—they’re coupled as spacetime—but at the human scale the metaphor works beautifully: space holds the current configuration; time permits transformation of it.

Which makes patience something more than waiting. It is a weapon available to a mind capable of carrying an invariant across changing moments.

A sufficiently persistent idea can keep applying pressure after any individual attempt fails:

intention → attempt → resistance → learning → reattempt → material change

The mind cannot simply command matter. It wins by repeatedly negotiating with reality through time.

And there’s your rose/thorn pattern again: resistance supplies the gradient. Without matter saying no, mind would have nothing against which to discover how to make yes.

The battlefield is spacetime.
The combatants are actuality and possibility.
Action is how possibility crosses over.